# Chapter 9: Equations.  The chapter is kept in four sources that this file
# reads: the editor, the display and the printing, the command words, and
# the errors, limits and examples.
.doc Strata: Equations
.chapter 9
.h1 Equations [equations]
.ix Equation
.ix Equations
.ix Equation marker
.ix TeX
.ix LaTeX
.ix Mathematics
An equation in Strata is a marker, like a note or a date, that stands in the
text where the formula goes.  Its content is the text of the formula written in
the math notation of TeX: {eq:x=\frac{{-b\pm\sqrt{{b^2-4ac}}}}{{2a}}} is the
text
{c:x=\frac{{-b\pm\sqrt{{b^2-4ac}}}}{{2a}}}.  The Editor typesets that text to a
picture in the X11 version and shows the picture in the document; the Printer
has LaTeX set it on the page.  The terminal version has no pictures and shows
the marker as a label with the text.

The equations of Strata are TeX math and nothing else.  A document that has
equations that are not TeX math must be rewritten, equation by equation, in TeX
math ({n:eq-legacy}); the Printer reports each such equation as an error.  This
chapter documents what Strata does with the text of an equation, and every
command word that the notation has in Strata, with the result.

The chapter is built as a reference:
.table 19 41
Section | What it holds
{x:eq-what} | What an equation is and what is needed to typeset it.
{x:eq-insert} | How to insert, edit, view, copy and delete an equation.
{x:eq-place} | Inline and displayed equations; the rule that determines.
{x:eq-screen} | How the equation is shown on the screen (pictures, labels,
 display modes).
{x:eq-print} | How it is printed and what is refused.
{x:eq-text} | The rules for the text: spaces, groups, special characters.
{x:eq-greek} to {x:eq-structures} | The command words, one group at a time, in
 tables of source, result and meaning.
{x:eq-errors} | Every message, the lists of refused commands, the limits.
{x:eq-legacy} | Documents that have equations that are not TeX math.
{x:eq-examples} | Worked examples.
.end
The tables show the source of a formula next to the typeset result.  The result
is itself an equation of this document, so it is typeset by the same program
that is described here: in the printed manual by LaTeX, on the screen by the
pictures of {x:eq-screen}.  The Editor makes the pictures in the
background, so a screen of the later tables shows labels in place of the
results that are not ready yet; a picture that cannot be made keeps its label
({n:eq-screen-label}).  One session of the Editor keeps up to 4096 different
pictures ({n:eq-limits}).  The printed pages are not affected.

A formula is not the only way to reach what LaTeX can do.  For anything else
that the page needs, a LaTeX marker holds the instructions themselves
({n:marker-latex}).

The documentation has a card of the symbols: Math Symbols is one sheet of
paper, printed on both sides and folded in three, that shows every symbol and
every construct that works in an equation, each as it is printed and with the
text to type beside it.  It is a Strata document, and its symbols are
equations.

.include equations-a.src
.include equations-t.src
.include equations-d.src
